WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.920
Stripe's bet that cheaper coding means more software, not fewer builders.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:16.720
Plus, Laura Modi's crash course inside Google Finance, the power struggle behind industrialization, and why psychiatry still lacks objective biomarkers.

3
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.960
On the A16Z podcast, Stripe's leadership argues that agentic coding shouldn't just cut costs.

4
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.160
It should make building software cheap enough to unleash a wave of new companies.

5
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.160
Timely, with Stripe reportedly pursuing open router for more than seven billion dollars.

6
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:52.720
And I think a lot of companies have skewed in that direction where they've seen this new, you know, agentic uh efficiency and power as a way to optimize cost structure.

7
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.760
Uh, you saw some layoffs, you saw you know, companies shrinking op X.

8
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:04.240
And our belief is that it's just an opportunity to, I'm being a little cheeky, but build everything.

9
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:04.720
Yes.

10
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.280
Yeah.

11
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.520
And, you know, uh, the best way to optimize your cost structure is to grow more.

12
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:09.840
Yes.

13
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:10.080
Exactly.

14
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:16.640
We have reams of user asks going back many years that are unmet, and so we just want to get through them all faster.

15
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:29.200
And so while we have, you know, more and more productivity from most senior engineers, if you want to ship more and build faster, you have to create founder-like agency inside your company.

16
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:36.160
And so the main things that are holding back our progress today are actually sort of back office things.

17
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.280
You know, we are merging so much more code than last year, and it is stressing every system.

18
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.800
It is stressing how do we get things into our seller systems, how do we get things onto our pricing page.

19
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.000
How do we actually bring things to market when we can't train sellers on them fast enough.

20
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:05.880
And so we are really trying at this stage to optimize every single phase of, we think of it as sort of the critical path in Stripe from the ideas behind product development, the user asks, all the way to products being in users' hands.

21
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.160
And so, you know, to your question of how do you go faster, it's really two things.

22
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.440
It's one, just how do you create that agency for everyone at Stripe?

23
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:23.080
How do you let everyone at Stripe be an auteur, like a founder, you know, a creative and not have them held back by the morass of you know centralized processes.

24
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:23.720
Yeah, exactly.

25
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.320
Exactly.

26
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:25.720
Exactly.

27
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:33.320
And on the other side, then it's how do you, you know, with your develop uh your dev prod team just make the tools better and better and better.

28
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.960
So we create something called Stripe Minions, which we've blogged about a little bit.

29
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:55.640
And, you know, of course we've got tons of developer tooling to help users iterate with agents, but minions uh we think of as you know the most important, like one of our most important metrics internally is how many PRs and what percentage of our PRs are created by minions.

30
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.440
And the reason for that is that minions are one shot.

31
00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:58.920
Yeah.

32
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:07.400
So you you give it a prompt, and you know, it's going to build it and then it's going to, you know, go through CI CD and all of testing.

33
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.160
And then you're gonna review it.

34
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.400
So you're not gonna iterate, you're not going to planning mode.

35
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.440
You're just gonna say, this is what I want, go do it.

36
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.400
And so we think, you know, that's kind of where the world is going.

37
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:25.160
You know, it's the the one shot or the glorified rel floop where you're just sort of letting the agent be super, super powerful.

38
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:33.160
And so we blogged about minions, I think in January, February, and they were doing 1,200, you know, PRs per week.

39
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.480
And last week, I think 7,000 PRs came from minions.

40
00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:37.720
Wow.

41
00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:37.960
Yeah.

42
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.720
And about 30% of our PRs in that week came from minions.

43
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:52.400
So, you know, that sort of developer tooling and then the real bet is that lower costs expand ambition, not just margins.

