00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:13.000
On the Peter Atia Drive, psychiatrist Linus Abrams examines a basic limitation of his field.

91
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.840
Diagnoses still rely heavily on symptoms without objective biomarkers.

92
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:49.560
Is to really get to know people in depth who are remarkable human beings.

93
00:09:49.560 --> 00:10:14.040
And that's what inspired me to go into psychiatry in the first place, because understanding the patient is always greater than my ability or any provider to use that word, any provider's ability to understand them, because the nature of a human being is so unique and remarkable.

94
00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:27.880
So it's progressive layers of insight and progressive attempts to understand and reinterpret or misinterpretations, if you will.

95
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:48.640
I mean, one of the things about psychiatry that is quite unique to medicine when you consider all of the medical subspecialties and the surgical subspecialties, is the lack of measurable biomarkers or objective findings that could be measured on imaging, for example.

96
00:10:49.120 --> 00:11:05.120
So when you think through presumably the spectrum of conditions that a psychiatrist would be treating from anxiety to depression to hypomania, bipolar disorder, all of the different clustered all of these things.

97
00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:10.080
There's nothing that's going to show up on a CT scan or an MRI of the brain that makes that diagnosis.

98
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:16.320
There isn't a blood-based biomarker that's going to say, oh, this, you know, this person has high ferritin or this person has low this or low that.

99
00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:16.960
Not yet.

100
00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:17.360
Yep.

101
00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:33.200
So yet, in other words, something you said a moment ago rings very important, which is even though you you've used the term psychopharmacologist several times already, implying that the tool, the main tool you use is a pharmacologic tool.

102
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:38.560
The human story piece of it is the diagnosis, right?

103
00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:48.240
I mean, presumably part of the diagnosis is ratified through a hypothesis of, hey, I think if if my if I'm right on the diagnosis, this medication should make things a little bit better.

104
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:52.640
But you have to have a very good hypothesis based on your subjective interaction.

105
00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:59.120
That gap shapes both how mental illness is measured and what counts as recovery.

